[Music]  america  lost a legend today baseball's former  home run king hameron hank aaron died  overnight  at age 86. jackie robinson opened the  door for black major league baseball  players  but aaron busted it down after a short  stint in the negro leagues the mobile  alabama native  entered major league baseball with the  milwaukee braves in 1954.  the team would move to atlanta in 1966  over his 23-year career and aaron would  become one of the game's premier stars  raking up countless honors including a  world series title  and nvp award in 1957 but his crowning  achievement was breaking babe ruth's  career home run record  it happened on this swing in 1974.  much like jackie robinson three decades  earlier america's  ugly history of racism also affected  aaron  he received hate mail and death threats  as he chased ruth's record  today show anchor craig melvin talked to  aaron about that  in an interview last year  i was surprised to read in what should  have been  the most exciting period of your life  you're chasing babe ruth's record it  wasn't it was not  why not i couldn't go out of the  ballpark without  an escort i had to stay in another hotel  rather than staying in a hotel with my  teammates  it was the toughest moment of my life  joining me now is a man who interviewed  hank aaron several times over  the years mlv.com columnist mike lupica  is back with us first off it's just  amazing to think about the legacy of  hank aaron i grew up  with a dad um who loved hank aaron and  loves baseball so  um when i heard this news today i was  incredibly sad but give me a sense  of the impact of hank aaron and the  impact he had on baseball but also on  america  yeah that's the thing zulina you know  hank aaron i i tweeted this out today  um he was not just hank aaron great  baseball player  he was hank aaron great american this  was an extraordinary  american life i i wrote about this today  he was born two years into fdr's first  term  and lived long enough to see joe biden  get sworn into office  this week that's the stretch and the  scope of this man's life  he was a gentle strong elegant  graceful gifted person he played for the  indianapolis  clowns in the negro leagues for 200  a month in 1953. he said he had to make  a jar of peanut butter go a long  long way and then his original contract  got sold to the boston braves  for ten thousand dollars and and the  rest is a glorious history  of of of this sport but again this child  of mobile alabama  ended up breaking the record of babe  ruth in atlanta so a child of the south  and and when he talks about the hate  mail he got i spoke to bud selig today  his dear friend for 60 years the former  commissioner of baseball  he saw those letters and i asked mr  aaron one time  i said why did you keep him and he said  because they were part of my story  too and what part of what he told craig  today he  he not only had to sneak out of  ballparks when he's tracing  the most famous record in the history of  american sports he said he  slept in ballpark some nights just to be  safe  wow wow that's incredible um and you got  to interview him a few times just in the  last year  which is amazing i wanted to read a  quick quote from aaron that  struck me from your conversations he  said i was thinking later on when pete  rose was  trying to break ty cobb's all-time  record how much people love  pete and we're rooting for him i wish i  could have had  a little of that love what's your  reaction to that when you when you talk  about  the fact that he has to sleep in the in  the stadium  uh for his own safety you know it's such  a shame when you think about  other players later on in history that  uh  received accolades at the time they were  breaking records  yeah i mean it should have been a joyful  pageant  of american sports and the guy and he  said to me once  i didn't think of myself as trying to  break babe ruth's record  i was trying to set my own and  and and our last conversation four days  before christmas we were talking about  how  negro league um stats had now been added  to the history of baseball  and you know he only needed a handful to  have ended up with more than barry bonds  with later on  and he said tell them that if they can  find nine or ten home runs of mine  i'll i'll take them and he was he had  the most  wonderful disposition and selena you  know if  it's obvious that you've seen these  columns we had a long and i felt  important conversation about george  floyd in the shadow of the death  of george floyd and and imagine now  the the life hank aaron led and what he  had gone through  and he said at the time i'm frightened  for america this man was afraid of  nothing  he was afraid of nobody he said the  conversations  that he once had with his grandchildren  had come rushing back to him  and and at the end of one of those  columns he said to me  i don't get around much anymore he he  was in a wheelchair mostly for the last  few years of his life  and he said but if i could i'd be at the  front of those lines  and and i you know what he would have  been in the front of those lines  uh last spring when he thought there  might not be a baseball season  at the end of one of our conversations  had a really soft voice  he said god i love baseball and that was  the end of my column today for mlb  i said that was god's gift to baseball  talk into the world  in terms of you know how much he cared  about the community obviously you talked  to him  uh in the aftermath of george floyd but  also we showed earlier that his very  last  post on twitter was him getting the  vaccine really just trying to inspire  communities of color  to do the same why was it so important  for him  not just to be good at sports but to  also be a role model  for communities of color and and really  you know young kids writ large  he he was so profoundly blessed in his  mind that that  the lord had given him this talent to  play baseball that jackie robinson had  blazed the trail  for him in 1947 and and i can't tell you  how many times he told me  i believe it is my duty to pay it  forward he helped raise  millions of dollars for the united negro  college fund he  he was he was a he was a passionate  person about social justice  and again so much of the racism that we  see now  in this country he lived it in his  pursuit of  babe ruth 36 you know in 1974.  i mean it's it's it's it's it's  remarkable nothing that was happening in  this country  over the past couple of years surprised  him at all he knew from his own life  that race was and always would be the  third rail in this  in this country  do you feel like as you talked to him in  the last year  that he felt optimistic about the future  obviously it's hard to say because  uh you know donald trump was the  president for the last four years but  he did get to see at least you know a  little bit of a shift post  november and i feel like um you know  somebody who's lived through the  experiences that you just detailed  you know inside they are optimistic they  have to be to just continue to get up  every single day  uh and engage uh you know so uh  intensely in all of these issues  oh no he he he was talking about how  how excited he was again in the shadow  of the death of george floyd  how much he loved seeing kids in the  streets  white kids black kids he said it didn't  matter the color  social justice in in his mind at that  point didn't require  a a test to see what color you were and  so that he was  incredibly optimistic and again  i'm so glad he got to see last week  in baseball i i hope he was alert and  aware  and saw the way this country felt  at noon on wednesday when when joe biden  got sworn in  absolutely it was history uh seeing  kamala harris sworn in as well was  was it put a smile on so many faces  around the country because  of um a first you know it's hard to be a  first tank aaron and jackie robinson  prove that  um but it's so amazing when you get  there mike lupica thank you so much for  being here  um i was incredibly excited to have you  tonight  for this conversation even though it is  sad news it's important to reflect on an  uh incredible legacy  hi i'm zerlina maxwell thanks for  checking out our channel on youtube  you can see more from zerlina by  clicking any of the videos on this  screen  and make sure you subscribe below to  stay up to date on the day's biggest  stories  thanks for watching

